  (314, 'A male platypus has venomous spurs on its hind legs capable of causing excruciating pain in humans.'),
  (315, 'The eye of a giant squid can be up to 10 inches across — the largest of any living animal.'),
  (316, 'Strawberries contain more vitamin C than oranges by weight.'),
  (317, 'Ants stretch and yawn when they wake up, much like humans.'),
  (318, 'In some countries, it''s illegal to name your child "Ikea" or "Elvis." France banned the name "Nutella."'),
  (319, 'Honey is the only food that includes all the substances necessary to sustain life, including water.'),
  (320, 'A human could theoretically walk to the Moon in about nine years.'),
  (321, 'Lobsters were once so plentiful in colonial America that they were fed to prisoners as "poverty food."'),
  (322, 'The average reader''s eyes move across a page in saccadic jumps, not in a smooth sweep.'),
  (323, 'In 1961, a US nuclear bomb was accidentally dropped over North Carolina. It nearly detonated — five of its six safety switches had been triggered.'),
  (324, 'A person dies from not sleeping before they die from not eating — you''d perish from sleep deprivation in about 11 days.'),
  (325, 'You share 50% of your DNA with a banana and 98% with a chimpanzee.'),
  (326, 'The planet Earth weighs approximately 5.97 × 10^24 kg — but you can''t "weigh" it in the traditional sense, as there''s no scale to put it on.'),
  (327, 'Pterodactyls are not dinosaurs — they are a different clade of archosaurs called pterosaurs.'),
  (328, 'The odds of being dealt a royal flush in poker are approximately 1 in 649,740.'),
  (329, 'Turtles breathe through their butts — technically through a process called cloacal bursae respiration.'),
  (330, 'The word "volcano" comes from Vulcan, the Roman god of fire.'),
  (331, 'Giraffes hum to each other at night at a frequency too low for humans to hear.'),
  (332, 'A "butt" is an actual unit of measurement — it refers to 108 gallons of wine.'),
  (333, 'Scotland has 421 words for snow in its native Scots language.'),
  (334, 'The Atacama Desert in Chile is the driest non-polar desert on Earth — some areas have never recorded rain.'),
  (335, 'Honey bees beat their wings 200 times per second.'),
  (336, 'You can''t sneeze with your eyes open — the reflex is involuntary.'),
  (337, 'The first space tourist was Dennis Tito in 2001, who paid $20 million for a trip to the ISS.'),
  (338, 'Coffee was discovered — according to legend — when an Ethiopian goat herder noticed his goats became lively after eating certain berries.'),
  (339, 'Peanuts are not nuts — they are legumes, in the same family as beans and lentils.'),
  (340, 'A single lightning bolt contains enough energy to power a 60-watt light bulb for six months.'),
  (341, 'Killer whales (orcas) are not whales — they''re the largest members of the dolphin family.'),
  (342, 'Adult humans have 206 bones, but babies have around 270 — they fuse together as we grow.'),
  (343, 'Aluminum was once more valuable than gold — Napoleon III served honored guests with aluminum utensils while others used gold.'),
  (344, 'Charles Darwin and Abraham Lincoln were born on the exact same day — February 12, 1809.'),
  (345, 'Pigs are among the smartest animals in the world, ranking above dogs in many cognitive tests.'),
  (346, 'Your fingernails grow faster on your dominant hand and faster in summer than in winter.'),
  (347, 'Polar bears are left-handed — or rather, left-pawed.'),
  (348, 'In 1917, Finland used squirrel pelts as currency.'),
  (349, 'Every time you lick a stamp, you consume one-tenth of a calorie.'),
  (350, 'The word "avocado" comes from the Aztec Nahuatl word "āhuacatl," meaning "testicle."'),
  (351, 'An owl can''t move its eyeballs — instead, it rotates its entire head up to 270 degrees.'),
  (352, 'The tongue print of every person is unique, like fingerprints.'),
  (353, 'The longest an elephant has been observed to grieve is three weeks — they will return to the bones of deceased family members.'),
  (354, 'Water striders can "see" with their legs — their leg hairs sense vibrations in the water.'),
  (355, 'An astronaut''s height increases by up to two inches in space because the spine expands without gravity.'),
  (356, 'The smell of freshly cut grass is the plant releasing chemicals to signal distress to neighboring plants.'),
  (357, 'The human jaw is the strongest muscle in the body per unit area.'),
  (358, 'Octopuses have been filmed unscrewing jars from the inside.'),
  (359, 'Black holes don''t "suck" things in — their gravity attracts surrounding matter the same way any massive object would.'),
  (360, 'Almonds are in the same genus as apricots, cherries, and plums.'),
  (361, 'A flock of geese on the ground is a gaggle; in flight, it becomes a skein.'),
  (362, 'The human brain is about 75% water.'),
  (363, 'Tardigrades (water bears) can survive in the vacuum of space, extreme radiation, and temperatures near absolute zero.'),
  (364, 'The Moon has "moonquakes" that can last for more than 10 minutes.'),
  (365, 'On the Moon, the footprints made by Apollo astronauts will remain visible for roughly 100 million years.'),
  (366, 'Sea cucumbers breathe through their anus.'),
  (367, 'You lose around 4 kilograms of skin cells each year.'),
  (368, 'The human eye is sensitive enough to detect a single photon in complete darkness.'),
  (369, 'No number before 1,000 contains the letter "a."'),
  (370, 'A ball of chocolate can be heard unwrapping at the same frequency as a chocolate factory''s machinery, which scientists discovered was meaningful.'),
  (371, 'The blob fish (Psychrolutes marcidus) only looks blobby when brought to the surface — at depth, it''s a fairly normal-looking fish.'),
  (372, 'The word "lollipop" possibly comes from Romany (Gypsy) for "red apple."'),
  (373, 'Switzerland didn''t give women the right to vote in federal elections until 1971.'),
  (374, 'The first person to sail solo around the world was Joshua Slocum, finishing in 1898.'),
  (375, 'A group of lemurs is called a conspiracy.'),
  (376, 'It would take 76,000 years to travel to the nearest star system (Alpha Centauri) at the speed of the fastest spacecraft ever launched.'),
  (377, 'Humans are the only animals on Earth that blush.'),
  (378, 'The letter "Q" does not appear in any US state name.'),
  (379, 'The letter "B" does not appear in any number name until "billion."'),
  (380, 'Honey bees can recognize human faces as unique visual patterns.'),
  (381, 'Glass is not a liquid — despite the old myth, antique windows are thicker at the bottom because of old manufacturing processes, not because glass "flows."'),
  (382, 'Crows have demonstrated the ability to solve eight-step puzzles to reach food.'),
  (383, 'The human tongue has about 10,000 taste buds.'),
  (384, 'An elephant''s pregnancy lasts 22 months — the longest of any land mammal.'),
  (385, 'Beethoven wrote his Ninth Symphony — including the famous "Ode to Joy" — while completely deaf.'),
  (386, 'The dot on the letter "i" is called a "tittle." The crossbar on a "t" is called a "tittle" too, technically.'),
  (387, 'The average American consumes 17 teaspoons of added sugar per day — more than double the recommended amount.'),
  (388, 'In Montana, there are three times as many cows as people.'),
  (389, 'Squirrels plant tens of thousands of trees every year by forgetting where they buried nuts.'),
  (390, 'Ants have two stomachs — one for their own food, one for sharing with the colony.'),
  (391, 'Snakes have two penises (called hemipenes) but only use one at a time.'),
  (392, 'If the history of Earth were compressed into 24 hours, humans would appear in the last two seconds.'),
  (393, 'Jupiter''s red spot is a storm that has been raging for at least 350 years — it''s larger than Earth.'),
  (394, 'Lobsters don''t die of old age — they theoretically keep growing indefinitely if not eaten or diseased.'),
  (395, 'The Moon is drifting away from the Earth by about 1.5 inches per year.'),
  (396, 'There are roughly 70 sextillion stars in the observable universe — that''s 7 followed by 22 zeros.'),
  (397, 'Crows can use traffic to crack nuts — they place them on roads and wait for cars to run them over.'),
  (398, 'A cubic centimeter of a neutron star weighs about one billion tons.'),
  (399, 'The fastest thing in the universe is the expansion of space itself — parts of the universe are expanding faster than light.'),
  (400, 'The first webcam was invented to watch a coffee pot at the University of Cambridge.'),
  (401, 'A shark must keep moving to breathe, but a nurse shark can remain motionless.'),
  (402, 'The longest wedding veil on record was as long as 63.5 football fields.'),
  (403, 'Kangaroos can''t move their hind legs independently — they always hop with both legs together.'),
  (404, 'The Great Pyramid of Giza is the oldest of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World and the only one still largely intact.'),
  (405, 'There have been 108 confirmed meteorite impacts on Earth, but thousands more likely went undetected.'),
  (406, 'Every year, billions of carrier pigeons were used to deliver messages in WWI — they had a remarkable 95% success rate.'),
  (407, 'The original Sherlock Holmes stories were written by Arthur Conan Doyle, who actually hated his most famous character.'),
  (408, 'In 2007, an 8th-century Viking sword was found by a child in a Swedish lake — completely intact.'),
  (409, 'The human body contains about 100,000 miles of blood vessels — enough to circle the Earth four times.'),
  (410, 'A chameleon''s tongue is twice the length of its body and accelerates from 0 to 60 mph in a hundredth of a second.'),
  (411, 'Mars appears red because of rust — its soil contains iron oxide.'),
  (412, 'Cheetahs are the only big cats that can purr.'),
  (413, 'Cats can make over 100 different vocalizations; dogs can only make about 10.'),
  (414, 'Sloths have such slow metabolisms they can survive a month on just a few leaves.'),
