  (586, 'More than half of all known species on Earth are insects.'),
  (587, 'An alligator can grow a new tooth to replace any tooth lost — up to 50 times in a lifetime.'),
  (588, 'The Sun accounts for about 99.86% of all the mass in our solar system.'),
  (589, 'Butterflies navigate partly by sensing the Earth''s magnetic field.'),
  (590, 'Horses can''t breathe through their mouths — they are obligate nasal breathers.'),
  (591, 'No piece of paper can be folded in half more than 7 times by hand, though machines have done it more.'),
  (592, 'The average American spends five years of their life eating.'),
  (593, 'Coconut water can be used as blood plasma in emergency situations — it is sterile and isotonic.'),
  (594, 'The phrase "the real McCoy" comes from the inventor Elijah McCoy, whose genuine products were so superior that people started asking for "the real McCoy."'),
  (595, 'Cats have 32 muscles in each ear, allowing them to rotate them independently by 180 degrees.'),
  (596, 'There are more than 150,000 species of fungi on Earth — only about 1,000 are known to be dangerous.'),
  (597, 'A hippopotamus produces its own sunscreen — a reddish oily substance secreted from its skin.'),
  (598, 'Humans are the only animals on Earth that cry emotional tears.'),
  (599, 'The human stomach acid is strong enough to dissolve stainless steel.'),
  (600, 'There are more types of beetles than any other order of animal — about 400,000 known species.'),
  (601, 'The first alarm clock could only ring at one fixed time — it couldn''t be set.'),
  (602, 'Blue-ringed octopuses are the size of a golf ball but carry enough venom to kill 26 humans in minutes.'),
  (603, 'Polar bear fur absorbs UV light and can be used as a model for more efficient solar energy collectors.'),
  (604, 'A group of wolves is called a pack; a group of foxes is called a skulk or earth.'),
  (605, 'African elephants can detect water underground and will dig holes with their tusks to reach it.'),
  (606, 'The first computer virus was created in 1983 by Fred Cohen as a research experiment.'),
  (607, 'In a phenomenon called "quantum tunneling," particles can pass through solid objects — without this, the Sun wouldn''t shine.'),
  (608, 'Coconuts kill more people each year than sharks.'),
  (609, 'It takes approximately 12 years for a single can of aluminum to decompose.'),
  (610, 'Only two mammals lay eggs: the platypus and the echidna.'),
  (611, 'Crocodilians are more closely related to birds than to lizards.'),
  (612, 'A snail''s mouth is smaller than a pin but contains up to 25,000 tiny teeth.'),
  (613, 'All clownfish are born male — the dominant fish in a group will change to female if needed.'),
  (614, 'The word "disaster" comes from Greek and Latin roots meaning "bad star" — ill-fated celestial position.'),
  (615, 'In 2005, scientists created a molecule that was 5 atoms wide and used it to record the world''s smallest movie.'),
  (616, 'Greenland is three times the size of the US state of Texas.'),
  (617, 'Wolves can hear other wolves howling from 10 miles away.'),
  (618, 'The world''s fastest land animal — the cheetah — loses most races it enters because it tires after 20–30 seconds.'),
  (619, 'Sound cannot travel through the vacuum of space — space is completely silent.'),
  (620, 'The oldest living thing on Earth is a clonal colony of quaking aspen trees in Utah called "Pando," estimated at about 80,000 years old.'),
  (621, 'The Sun is so large that 1.3 million Earths could fit inside it.'),
  (622, 'The wingspan of a Boeing 747 is longer than the Wright Brothers'' first flight.'),
  (623, 'Ancient Romans used their urine as a fabric softener and bleach for laundry.'),
  (624, 'The first movie ever made was "Roundhay Garden Scene" in 1888 — it lasts about 2 seconds.'),
  (625, 'Male giraffes fight by swinging their necks like wrecking balls — this is called "necking."'),
  (626, 'Goats have rectangular pupils for a nearly panoramic field of vision to spot predators.'),
  (627, 'Bananas are radioactive due to the presence of potassium-40.'),
  (628, 'It is impossible to fold a piece of paper in half more than 7 times by hand, regardless of size.'),
  (629, 'In 2006, a Chinese student discovered a 47-million-year-old spider fossil in the act of attacking a wasp — perfectly preserved in amber.'),
  (630, 'Tortoises can live to over 180 years — Harriet, a tortoise believed to have been collected by Charles Darwin, died in 2006 at approximately 175.'),
  (631, 'Coyotes have been observed teaching their pups to avoid roads with traffic.'),
  (632, 'A group of pandas is called an embarrassment.'),
  (633, 'The total amount of DNA in a single human body, if stretched out, would reach from Earth to Pluto and back — several times.'),
  (634, 'Ancient Greeks didn''t distinguish between blue and green, using the same word for both.'),
  (635, 'Each square inch of human skin contains about 32 million bacteria.'),
  (636, 'Sharks can feel a single drop of blood diluted in 25 gallons of water.'),
  (637, 'It is not possible to lick your elbow — most people instinctively try when told this, which is the joke.'),
  (638, 'You can hear the sound of your own heartbeat more clearly in total silence, which is why some people find silence unsettling.'),
  (639, 'The Hubble Space Telescope generates about 10 terabytes of new data every year.'),
  (640, 'In ancient China, soldiers sometimes wore armor made of silk, which was found to be more resistant to arrows than metal.'),
  (641, 'Frogs don''t drink water — they absorb it through their skin.'),
  (642, 'A blue whale can eat up to 4 tons of krill in a single day.'),
  (643, 'Bald eagles are not bald — the name comes from the Old English "balde," meaning "white."'),
  (644, 'Dogs can detect their owner''s smell even when it''s diluted one million times.'),
  (645, 'In 1919, a molasses tank in Boston exploded, sending a wave of molasses 25 feet high through the streets at 35 mph, killing 21 people.'),
  (646, 'The total weight of all insects on Earth is estimated to outweigh the total weight of all other animals combined.'),
  (647, 'Koalas have one of the smallest brain-to-body ratios of any mammal — their brain only fills about 61% of their skull.'),
  (648, 'Trees are the largest living things on Earth by mass. The largest known individual tree is "Hyperion," a coastal redwood, at 380 feet tall.'),
  (649, 'Some fish can walk on land — mudskippers, for instance, use their pectoral fins like arms.'),
  (650, 'In 2016, a Japanese convenience store chain launched a chocolate-covered potato chip — it became a runaway hit worldwide.'),
  (651, 'Elephants are capable of basic arithmetic — they can understand the concept of "one more" in experiments.'),
  (652, 'The Moon has a weaker gravitational pull than Earth, so an astronaut on the Moon can jump 6 times higher than on Earth.'),
  (653, 'Flamingos can live up to 40 years in the wild and up to 60 in captivity.'),
  (654, 'There is a jellyfish called Turritopsis dohrnii that can revert to its juvenile form after sexual maturity, potentially making it biologically immortal.'),
  (655, 'The world''s oldest library still in operation is the Library of Al-Qarawiyyin in Morocco, founded in 859 CE.'),
  (656, 'A crocodile can hold its breath underwater for up to two hours.'),
  (657, 'Stars twinkle because their light passes through Earth''s atmosphere; planets don''t twinkle because they''re close enough to appear as discs rather than points of light.'),
  (658, 'The first iPhone was released in 2007 — at the time, it cost $499 for 4GB and $599 for 8GB.'),
  (659, 'There are more ways to arrange a deck of 52 cards than there are atoms on Earth.'),
  (660, 'The oldest known piece of art is a carved ochre in South Africa, dated to approximately 73,000 years ago.'),
  (661, 'Some species of ants practice "ant farming" — they cultivate aphids for honeydew the same way humans farm cows for milk.'),
  (662, 'Broccoli contains twice as much vitamin C as an orange.'),
  (663, 'Cats spend about 70% of their lives asleep.'),
  (664, 'The average person has about 1,460 dreams per year — but forgets the vast majority.'),
  (665, 'The average person produces enough earwax in a lifetime to fill a small glass.'),
  (666, 'In Japan, the Kit Kat brand is hugely popular because "Kitto Katsu" means "you will surely win" in Japanese — making them popular exam gifts.'),
  (667, 'Male bees (drones) exist only to mate with the queen — after which they die or are expelled from the hive.'),
  (668, 'The longest recorded time a person has stayed awake is about 264 hours (11 days), by Randy Gardner in 1964.'),
  (669, 'In Norway, salmon can legally be classified as a vegetable under food labeling regulations for certain processed products.'),
  (670, 'A hummingbird''s heart beats up to 1,200 times per minute during flight.'),
  (671, 'Honey bees communicate the location of flowers through a "waggle dance" — a figure-eight movement that encodes direction and distance.'),
  (672, 'The Eiffel Tower was the tallest man-made structure in the world from 1889 until 1930.'),
  (673, 'Penguins propose with rocks — male Adélie penguins select the best pebble from the beach and present it to potential mates.'),
  (674, 'Tomatoes originally came from South America and were considered poisonous in Europe for a time.'),
  (675, 'Human babies are born with 300 bones; adults have 206 — many fuse during childhood.'),
  (676, 'In 2017, scientists discovered a new organ in the human body: the mesentery, which connects the intestine to the abdominal wall.'),
  (677, 'Africa is the only continent that spans all four hemispheres — north, south, east, and west.'),
  (678, 'Honey has been found in Egyptian tombs over 3,000 years old — and was still perfectly edible.'),
  (679, 'A full moon during winter is brighter than during summer because snow reflects more light.'),
  (680, 'Alligators are more closely related to birds and dinosaurs than to modern reptiles like lizards.'),
  (681, 'Fireflies communicate with bioluminescence — each species has a unique flash pattern to attract mates.'),
  (682, 'All the gold mined in history could be compacted into a cube about 21 meters on each side.'),
  (683, 'Sand dollars are alive when found on the beach. If they have any color, they''re alive. White ones are dead.'),
  (684, 'Octopuses have been observed playing — throwing objects through water repeatedly just for fun.'),
  (685, 'Gorillas can catch human colds and other illnesses — because of our close genetic relationship.'),
  (686, 'An octopus''s suction cups can taste and feel simultaneously.'),
  (687, 'Koalas'' closest relative isn''t the bear — it''s the wombat.'),
  (688, 'The word "muscle" originates from the Latin "mus" (mouse) because ancient Romans saw the shape of a flexing bicep as a mouse moving under skin.'),
  (689, 'Australia has 20 out of the world''s 25 most venomous snakes.'),
  (690, 'The world''s loudest bird is the white bellbird, whose call can reach 125 decibels — as loud as a rock concert.'),
  (691, 'Dolphins can call each other by "name" — each dolphin has a unique signature whistle used for individual identification.'),
  (692, 'In ancient Rome, only the emperor could wear purple — the dye was incredibly rare and valuable.'),
  (693, 'The average American will eat about 35,000 cookies in their lifetime.'),
  (694, 'A blue whale''s call is louder than a jet engine and can be heard 1,000 miles away.'),
  (695, 'Each human fingerprint is unique — even identical twins have different fingerprints.'),
  (696, 'Crows have episodic memory — they remember specific past events and can plan based on them.'),
  (697, 'Earthworms can regenerate their tail end if cut, but not their head end.'),
  (698, 'Einstein''s eyes are kept in a safe in New York City — his ophthalmologist removed them after his death and kept them.'),
  (699, 'The Amazon Rainforest creates its own rainfall — trees release water vapor that seeds new clouds and rain.'),
  (700, 'Jupiter has 95 known moons as of 2024 — more than any other planet in the solar system.'),
  (701, 'A day on Jupiter is only about 10 hours long.'),
  (702, 'It takes light from the Andromeda Galaxy 2.5 million years to reach us — when you look at it, you''re seeing it as it was 2.5 million years ago.'),
  (703, 'The ocean covers 71% of the Earth''s surface.'),
  (704, 'Flamingos can drink boiling hot water — some populations survive around volcanic hot springs.'),
  (705, 'Mercury is both the smallest planet and the second densest in our solar system.'),
  (706, 'Jellyfish have been on Earth for over 500 million years — predating dinosaurs by over 250 million years.'),
  (707, 'A group of badgers is called a cete.'),
  (708, 'The first person to run a mile in under 4 minutes was Roger Bannister in 1954. His record was broken just 46 days later.'),
  (709, 'Dolphins have been observed using sponges as tools to protect their noses while foraging on the seafloor.'),
  (710, 'The collective intelligence of an ant colony is comparable to a simple neural network — together they solve problems no individual ant could.'),
  (711, 'Goats were the first animals to be domesticated for milk production.'),
  (712, 'The average human heart beats about 100,000 times per day.'),
  (713, 'In Victorian England, taxidermy of cats in novelty poses was an actual art form.'),
  (714, 'Sharks existed before Mount Everest was formed.'),
  (715, 'The human eye can detect a single candle flame from about 1.6 miles away in total darkness.'),
  (716, 'The country of Bhutan measures its success by "Gross National Happiness" rather than GDP.'),
  (717, 'Llamas can be trained as guard animals — they naturally bond with flocks and alert to predators.'),
  (718, 'The average person will spend 25 years asleep over the course of a lifetime.'),
  (719, 'Grizzly bears hibernate for up to 7 months without eating, drinking, or defecating.'),
  (720, 'Wombat poop is cube-shaped — the wombat produces up to 100 cubic droppings per night.'),
  (721, 'Humans are the only species known to have a chin — other apes don''t have them.'),
  (722, 'A single queen bee can lay up to 2,000 eggs per day.'),
  (723, 'The platypus can detect electrical fields from prey using electroreceptors in its bill.'),
  (724, 'Dung beetles navigate using the Milky Way — they use starlight to roll their balls of dung in a straight line.'),
  (725, 'Hippopotamuses spend most of their time in water, but they can''t actually swim — they walk on the riverbed.'),
  (726, 'Astronauts can''t burp in space — without gravity, gas can''t separate from liquid in the stomach.'),
  (727, 'A tiger can consume up to 88 pounds of meat in one sitting, then go without food for several days.'),
  (728, 'The fear of long words in psychology is called "hippopotomonstrosesquippedaliophobia" — a notoriously ironic term.'),
  (729, 'No two snowflakes are exactly alike, because their six-sided lattice structure is determined by infinitely variable conditions as they fall.'),
  (730, 'Lobsters taste with their legs, which have chemoreceptors that can detect dissolved particles in water.'),
  (731, 'In Iceland, it''s common to list books alphabetically by first name because Icelanders don''t use family surnames in the same way.'),
  (732, 'The word "nausea" comes from the Greek "naus," meaning ship — related to the nausea of being seasick.'),
  (733, 'Venus is the hottest planet in the solar system — hotter than Mercury, even though it''s farther from the Sun, due to its thick CO₂ atmosphere.'),
  (734, 'Dolphins will intentionally hyperventilate before diving to extend their dive time.'),
  (735, 'The human brain is more active at night than during the day.'),
  (736, 'Weasels have such a fast metabolism they must eat roughly every hour or they die.'),
  (737, 'Giraffes and humans have the same number of neck bones — just very different sizes.'),
  (738, 'Raindrops aren''t actually teardrop-shaped — they''re spherical when small and hamburger-shaped when larger due to air resistance.'),
  (739, 'A woodpecker''s tongue wraps around its skull inside the head to cushion the brain during pecking.'),
  (740, 'Guinea pigs don''t have tails and were originally bred as a food source in South America.'),
  (741, 'Ancient Egyptians treated toothaches by placing a dead mouse against the sore area.'),
  (742, 'Cleopatra owned a palace larger than the Parthenon in Athens.'),
  (743, 'There are approximately 10 quadrillion (10,000,000,000,000,000) ants on Earth.'),
  (744, 'Dolphins and orcas have been observed apparently "playing" with fish before eating them.'),
  (745, 'Mercury is the only planet in the solar system without any natural moons.'),
  (746, 'A polar bear''s liver is so toxic it can kill humans — it contains dangerously high levels of vitamin A.'),
  (747, 'Bats are the only flying mammals; flying squirrels actually glide, not fly.'),
  (748, 'Clams are filter feeders and some species live to over 500 years.'),
  (749, 'Frogs have been found frozen alive in ice — some species survive freezing by replacing the water in their cells with glucose.'),
  (750, 'Not all deserts are hot — cold deserts exist in Antarctica, Greenland, and parts of Asia.'),
  (751, 'Sea lions can keep a beat to music — they were observed by scientists in 2013 bobbing their heads in time to music.'),
  (752, 'The world''s highest-paid athlete of all time, adjusting for inflation, was a Roman charioteer named Gaius Appuleius Diocles, who earned the equivalent of about $15 billion.'),
  (753, 'Humans are bioluminescent — we emit a very faint glow from our skin that is too dim for our eyes to detect.'),
  (754, 'Orangutans use leaves as umbrellas and gloves when handling thorny fruit.'),
  (755, 'The closest living relative to the T. rex is the chicken.'),
  (756, 'When you''re born, your eyes are almost 80% of their adult size, but your nose and ears never stop growing.'),
  (757, 'The platypus has no nipples — milk simply oozes from glands in the mother''s skin.'),
  (758, 'In 1977, a New Zealand fisherman caught a giant squid estimated at 14 meters long.'),
  (759, 'Elephants can recognize up to 30 family members'' voices even when out of sight.'),
  (760, 'Starfish can regenerate limbs — some species can regrow an entire body from just one limb.'),
  (761, 'Cats are genetically wired to "hunt" twice a day — corresponding roughly to dawn and dusk.'),
  (762, 'The word "quiz" was reportedly invented by a Dublin theatre owner who bet he could introduce a new word into the language within 48 hours, and had "quiz" chalked on walls around the city.'),
  (763, 'The world''s first toilet was invented in ancient Rome, along with a communal sponge-on-a-stick (the tersorium) shared by bathers.'),
  (764, 'Dolphins mourn their dead, carrying deceased calves for days.'),
  (765, 'The world''s largest insect by wingspan is the white witch moth, with a wingspan up to 28 cm (11 inches).'),
  (766, 'Camels can drink up to 30 gallons of water in just 13 minutes.'),
  (767, 'The entire planet''s human population could fit standing in an area the size of Los Angeles.'),
  (768, 'One million ants are born every single second somewhere in the world.'),
  (769, 'All humans alive today are descended from a single woman who lived about 150,000–200,000 years ago, known as "Mitochondrial Eve."'),
  (770, 'Blue-eyed people are all descended from a single ancestor who lived between 6,000 and 10,000 years ago.'),
  (771, 'Ostriches have the largest eggs of any living bird and the largest eyes of any land animal.'),
  (772, 'Reindeer eyes turn blue in winter to see better in low UV light.'),
  (773, 'Only female lions hunt — male lions primarily patrol territory and eat what the females catch.'),
  (774, 'There are more cell phone subscriptions than there are people on Earth.'),
  (775, 'Ants never sleep and can live for up to 30 years (queen ants).'),
  (776, 'The original Batmobile from the 1960s TV show sold at auction for $4.2 million in 2013.'),
  (777, 'There is a town in Canada called "Dildo" — locals strongly resist renaming it.'),
  (778, 'A single acre of soybeans can produce enough biodiesel to drive a car 23,000 miles.'),
  (779, 'Cats purr at a frequency that promotes bone healing — their purring falls between 25 and 150 Hz.'),
  (780, 'The longest escalator in the world is in the St. Petersburg Metro, running for 137 meters.'),
  (781, 'A "blue moon" is not actually blue — the term refers to the second full moon in a single calendar month.'),
  (782, 'Earth''s core is as hot as the surface of the Sun — approximately 5,400°C.'),
  (783, 'Humans have been domesticating chickens for about 8,000 years, making them one of the oldest domesticated animals.'),
  (784, 'The oldest confirmed pet was a cat buried alongside a human in Cyprus 9,500 years ago.'),
  (785, 'The skin of a polar bear is black, and its fur is transparent, but it appears white because it scatters light.'),
  (786, 'A baby blue whale gains about 200 pounds per day during its first year of life.'),
  (787, 'There is no way to know for certain how the universe ends, but current theories suggest it ends in a "Big Freeze," "Big Rip," or "Big Crunch."'),
  (788, 'The longest river in the world is the Nile, but it''s contested — the Amazon may be longer depending on how the source is defined.'),
  (789, 'The Eiffel Tower weighs approximately 7,300 tons.'),
  (790, 'Porcupines can float in water — their hollow quills are filled with air.'),
  (791, 'Every mammal takes approximately 21 seconds to urinate, regardless of bladder size.'),
  (792, 'A cloud can hold over a million pounds of water before precipitation begins.'),
  (793, 'The 10th President of the United States, John Tyler (born 1790), still had a living grandchild as of 2020.'),
  (794, 'The average head of a human hair contains enough tensile strength to hold up to 100 grams.'),
  (795, 'Some sharks must swim continuously or they sink and suffocate — the great white shark is one of these.'),
  (796, 'In space, metal pieces will spontaneously weld together if they touch — called "cold welding."'),
  (797, 'The first computer mouse was made of wood.'),
  (798, 'A shark loses about 30,000 teeth in a lifetime, continuously replacing them from rows behind.'),
  (799, 'Sloths can drop up to a third of their body weight in a single bathroom visit, which they do only once a week.'),
  (800, 'All lemons grown in the world are descended from a single hybrid — a cross between a bitter orange and a citron.'),
  (801, 'Koalas eat only eucalyptus leaves, which are toxic to most animals — koalas have a unique gut bacteria to detoxify them.'),
  (802, 'There is a word in Portuguese — "saudade" — for a deep longing for something you may never have again. It has no direct English translation.'),
  (803, 'Humans are among the best long-distance runners of any animal on Earth — we evolved to persistence-hunt by running prey to exhaustion.'),
  (804, 'The word "serendipity" was coined by Horace Walpole in 1754, based on a Persian fairy tale "The Three Princes of Serendip."'),
  (805, 'Trees communicate with each other through underground fungal networks, sharing nutrients and stress signals — sometimes called the "Wood Wide Web."'),
  (806, 'Octopuses have three hearts, nine brains, and blue blood (due to copper-based haemocyanin rather than iron-based hemoglobin).'),
  (807, 'A group of fish is called a school, but a group of sharks is a shiver.'),
  (808, 'The first speeding ticket was issued in 1896 to a man driving 8 mph in a 2 mph zone.'),
  (809, 'Male lions sleep 20 hours a day — more than any other large mammal.'),
  (810, 'Frogs and toads are not separate species — "toad" is a common name for dry-skinned, terrestrial frogs.'),
  (811, 'The oldest message in a bottle was found in 2018 — it had been drifting in the Indian Ocean for 132 years.'),
  (812, 'Humans and giraffes have the same number of neck vertebrae (7), but a giraffe''s can be up to 10 inches each.'),
  (813, 'Japan has more 7-Eleven stores than the United States, where the chain originated.'),
  (814, 'Some species of fungi are luminescent — they glow green in the dark.'),
  (815, 'An adult human body has about 37.2 trillion cells.'),
  (816, 'The largest organism ever recorded is a fungal network in Oregon covering 2,385 acres.'),
  (817, 'A whale''s blow (the spray it makes when surfacing) is distinctive per species — experts can identify the whale by its blow.'),
  (818, 'Hippos produce a blood-like red sweat that acts as sunscreen and antimicrobial agent.'),
  (819, 'The moon smells like gunpowder — astronauts who tracked in moon dust reported the smell after removing their helmets.'),
  (820, 'Glass catfish are nearly transparent — you can see their spine and organs through their skin.'),
  (821, 'Scientists found that ravens make a "hand wave" equivalent — they hold out objects to attract the attention of another raven they want to interact with.'),
  (822, 'One in five Nobel laureates has been born or raised in New York City.'),
  (823, 'Lobsters used to be so common in colonial America that you could walk along the beach and pick them up.'),
  (824, 'A group of flamingos takes flight in waves rather than all at once — they run together to generate momentum before lifting off.'),
  (825, 'Water from your tap in New York City takes up to nine years to filter through the soil of the Catskill Mountains.'),
  (826, 'Astronaut Ed White, the first American to walk in space, said he could hear his heartbeat in total silence in the void.'),
  (827, 'Aphids are born pregnant — a complex reproductive strategy where embryos are already inside embryos.'),
  (828, 'There is a cave in Vietnam (Hang Son Doong) so large it has its own weather system — complete with clouds and jungle.'),
  (829, 'Stegosaurus and T. rex are separated by about 80 million years of evolutionary history — nearly as long as the gap between T. rex and us.'),
  (830, 'Humans can only hear frequencies between 20 Hz and 20,000 Hz — dogs can hear up to 65,000 Hz.'),
  (831, 'Mercury''s surface temperature swings from -173°C at night to 427°C during the day.'),
  (832, 'People with anxiety experience physical sensations differently — they often interpret heartbeat awareness as danger.'),
  (833, 'The concept of zero as a number was independently invented in India, Babylon, and by the Maya.'),
  (834, 'Scientists can estimate a whale''s age by counting growth layers in its earwax.'),
  (835, 'Recycling a single aluminum can saves enough electricity to run a TV for three hours.'),
  (836, 'All mammals, from mice to elephants, have approximately the same number of heartbeats in a lifetime — about 1.5 billion.'),
  (837, 'A cubic mile of ordinary fog contains less than a gallon of water in droplet form.'),
  (838, 'The average person uses 80–100 gallons of water each day.'),
  (839, 'Language processing in the brain happens in two areas: Broca''s area (speech production) and Wernicke''s area (language comprehension).'),
  (840, 'In 2019, scientists took the first ever image of a black hole — it was 55 million light years away.'),
  (841, 'Warblers migrate 600 miles non-stop across the Gulf of Mexico twice a year, a journey taking about 20 hours of continuous flight.'),
  (842, 'The word "salary" comes from the Latin word for salt, which was once traded as currency.'),
  (843, 'In the deep ocean, bioluminescent creatures produce 90% of their energy as light, with almost no heat waste.'),
  (844, 'Crocodiles have outlived the dinosaurs and have remained largely unchanged for 200 million years.'),
  (845, 'Dogs can smell things at least 10,000 times better than humans, and up to 100,000 times in some estimates.'),
  (846, 'Almost all dogs that existed at the time of the Titanic''s sinking survived — three dogs were saved, all from first class.'),
  (847, 'Male sea horses hold pregnancies and can give birth to up to 2,000 young in a single birth.'),
  (848, 'A black hole one light-year across would have a mass roughly equivalent to a supernova.'),
  (849, 'The wingspan of a wandering albatross can reach 3.5 meters (over 11 feet) — the largest of any living bird.'),
  (850, 'Frogs'' tongues are attached at the front of the mouth, not the back — for better projection.'),
  (851, 'Female kangaroos have three vaginas and can pause pregnancies.'),
  (852, 'Giraffes have no voice box but can hum, grunt, and make other sounds at low frequencies.'),
  (853, 'Sand tiger sharks eat each other in the womb — only one or two pups survive, having eaten all the others.'),
  (854, 'The pattern of a tiger''s stripes is like a fingerprint — no two tigers have identical stripe patterns.'),
  (855, 'To make a pound of black pepper, a plant needs four to five years to mature.'),
  (856, 'There are more stars in the universe than grains of sand on all beaches on Earth.'),
  (857, 'In 2012, researchers taught rats to drive tiny cars, and the rats appeared to find the experience calming.'),
  (858, 'A jellyfish is 95% water.'),
  (859, 'Flies see in almost slow motion — they can detect light flickering at 250 frames per second (humans can only detect about 60).'),
  (860, 'Some trees communicate distress through underground mycelial networks faster than signals travel through the tree itself.'),
  (861, 'An adult has about 5 liters of blood in their body.'),
  (862, 'Migratory birds have magnetite crystals in their beaks that allow them to sense Earth''s magnetic field.'),
  (863, 'Sea horses swim upright and die from exhaustion in rough seas because they''re such poor swimmers.'),
  (864, 'The Sun loses about 4 million tons of mass every second through nuclear fusion.'),
  (865, 'The farthest object visible to the naked eye is the Andromeda Galaxy, about 2.5 million light-years away.'),
  (866, 'Monarch butterflies migrate up to 3,000 miles between Canada and Mexico — and the specific butterflies that return are not the ones that left.'),
  (867, 'A horse''s teeth take up more space in its head than its brain.'),
  (868, 'Some species of barnacles have the longest penis relative to body size of any animal — up to 50 times their body length.'),
  (869, 'The shortest war in recorded history was between Britain and Zanzibar on August 27, 1896, lasting between 38 and 45 minutes.'),
  (870, 'A flea can jump up to 150 times its own height — the equivalent of a human jumping over a 50-story building.'),
  (871, 'Honey bees pollinate approximately one-third of all food humans consume.'),
  (872, 'A "jiffy" was defined by physicist Gilbert Newton Lewis as the time it takes light to travel 1 centimeter.'),
  (873, 'Lobsters, crabs, and shrimp are so closely related to insects that biologists sometimes refer to insects as "terrestrial crustaceans."'),
  (874, 'Adult males of certain species of spider tie up females before mating, because females sometimes eat them afterward.'),
  (875, 'A starfish has no brain — its nervous system runs along its arms.'),
  (876, 'Crows and ravens can solve puzzles that require planning future steps, a cognitive skill once thought uniquely human.'),
  (877, 'Some deep-sea fish use bioluminescent lures to attract prey in the darkness of the abyss.'),
  (878, 'The Great Wall of China is not one continuous wall — it''s a series of walls built by different dynasties over centuries.'),
  (879, 'Some sharks have electroreceptors so sensitive they can detect the electrical field of a single heartbeat.'),
  (880, 'The world''s most venomous animal is the box jellyfish, capable of killing a human in minutes.'),
  (881, 'Cats land on their feet using a "righting reflex" that activates about 3 feet off the ground — but they can be injured falling short distances because they don''t have time to prepare.'),
  (882, 'At least 60% of the world''s languages will have fewer than 10,000 speakers by 2100.'),
  (883, 'Turkeys can blush — their faces turn red when excited, and blue when frightened or angry.'),
  (884, 'Giant pandas in the wild eat for 12–16 hours a day because bamboo is so low in nutrition.'),
  (885, 'More people die from selfies each year than from shark attacks.'),
  (886, 'Trees are the longest-lived individual organisms on Earth — some may live thousands of years.'),
  (887, 'Sharks can detect one part of blood per million parts of seawater from up to a quarter mile away.'),
  (888, 'The platypus produces milk with over 250 times the antibacterial properties of human milk.'),
  (889, 'In Japan, there''s a specific word, "komorebi," for sunlight filtering through leaves in a forest — there''s no direct equivalent in English.'),
  (890, 'Ants have been farming for over 50 million years. Humans have been farming for about 12,000 years.'),
  (891, 'The average commercial airliner flies at a height of about 35,000 feet — above 99% of Earth''s atmosphere.'),
  (892, 'Chimpanzees can learn sign language and have taught it to their offspring without human instruction.'),
  (893, 'There are approximately 4 quadrillion quadrillion bacteria on Earth.'),
  (894, 'Every two weeks on average, a language dies somewhere in the world.'),
  (895, 'A locust swarm can cover 460 square miles and eat more food in a day than 35,000 people.'),
  (896, 'Narwhals'' tusks are actually elongated canine teeth — spiraling in the same left-hand direction for all narwhals.'),
  (897, 'The Earth is about 4.5 billion years old — roughly one-third the age of the universe.'),
  (898, 'In the U.S., more people are injured by pens and pencils each year than by bullets.'),
  (899, 'When a queen bee dies, worker bees raise a new queen by feeding a larva "royal jelly" — a substance that changes its development entirely.'),
  (900, 'A dog''s sense of smell is estimated to be up to 100,000 times more sensitive than a human''s.'),
  (901, 'Hot water freezes faster than cold water under certain conditions — this has been demonstrated repeatedly but is not yet fully explained.'),
  (902, 'A bolt of lightning is 5 times hotter than the surface of the Sun.'),
  (903, 'A chameleon can move its eyes independently of each other, seeing in two different directions at once.'),
  (904, 'All the planets in the solar system rotate counterclockwise when viewed from above the Sun''s north pole, except Venus and Uranus.'),
  (905, 'Frogs cannot survive a salt water environment — fresh water is vital to their permeable skin.'),
  (906, 'The word "lunch" is related to "lump" — referring to a chunk of food eaten midday.'),
  (907, 'In 1916, a circus elephant named Mary was publicly hanged by crane in a small town in Tennessee for killing a handler.'),
  (908, 'Humans are the only species that cooks food — this is believed to be what drove the rapid expansion of the human brain.'),
  (909, 'An average person will grow 590 miles of hair in a lifetime.'),
  (910, 'Horseshoe crabs have been on Earth for about 450 million years and have barely changed — their blue blood is used to test for bacterial contamination in medical equipment.'),
  (911, 'One teaspoon of a neutron star would weigh about 10 million tons.'),
  (912, 'The first person to go over Niagara Falls in a barrel and survive was Annie Edson Taylor, a 63-year-old schoolteacher, in 1901.'),
  (913, 'Cows can walk up stairs but find it nearly impossible to walk back down.'),
  (914, 'In the USA, a "billion" is 1,000,000,000. In some countries, a "billion" still means a million millions (1,000,000,000,000).'),
  (915, 'The oldest piece of cheese ever found was approximately 3,200 years old, discovered in an Egyptian tomb.'),
  (916, 'A group of baboons is called a congress.'),
  (917, 'The Aztec word for chocolate is "xocolatl," meaning "bitter water."'),
  (918, 'Some species of male spiders pluck a "gift" web for a female, then tie her in silk to prevent her from escaping before mating.'),
  (919, 'The Earth''s crust averages about 30 km thick on land — but the Pacific Ocean floor is only about 5 km thick.'),
  (920, 'There are 12 volcanoes on Earth currently erupting right now, on average.'),
  (921, 'There are more ways to arrange 10 Lego bricks of the same color than there are atoms in the universe.'),
  (922, 'In Switzerland, cows are legally entitled to social contact — isolating a single cow is a violation of animal welfare law.'),
  (923, 'The shortest commercial flight still in regular operation connects Westray and Papa Westray in the Orkney Islands — the journey takes about 57 seconds.'),
  (924, 'Electric eels can generate up to 600 volts — enough to stun a horse.'),
  (925, 'Japan''s railway system is so punctual that the average delay across the entire network is less than one minute.'),
  (926, 'A single human hair is strong enough to hold a 100-gram weight without breaking.'),
  (927, 'The Voyager 1 probe is so far away it takes light about 22 hours to reach it from Earth.'),
  (928, 'Madagascar separated from Africa 160 million years ago, before humans evolved — all of its mammals arrived by "rafting" on vegetation.'),
  (929, 'Adult humans spend roughly six years of their lives dreaming.'),
  (930, 'The first ever computer game was "Tennis for Two," created in 1958 on an oscilloscope.'),
  (931, 'Butterflies have taste sensors in their feet — they know if a leaf is edible the moment they land on it.'),
  (932, 'Rabbits and hares can die of fright — a severe shock can trigger fatal cardiac arrest.'),
  (933, 'Humans are the only animal species that recognizes itself in the mirror (apart from great apes, elephants, dolphins, and magpies).'),
  (934, 'Termites eat wood twice as fast when listening to rock music.'),
  (935, 'There are more than 800 languages spoken in New York City, making it the most linguistically diverse city in the world.'),
  (936, 'A single strand of spider web silk, if stretched around the Earth at the equator, would weigh less than 500 grams.'),
  (937, 'Every mammal with a backbone has the same seven neck vertebrae, including the giraffe and the blue whale.'),
  (938, 'Bees can sting other bees — guard bees kill intruders from rival hives using their stingers.'),
  (939, 'The Mariana Trench is deep enough to submerge Mount Everest, with more than a mile to spare.'),
  (940, 'Hippos are herbivores but have the most powerful bite of any land animal.'),
  (941, 'Dragonflies have been flying for 300 million years — before there were even dinosaurs.'),
  (942, 'Japanese giant hornets kill approximately 50 people a year in Japan — more than any other animal in the country.'),
  (943, 'In 1969, the US military briefly considered a project called "Operation Acoustic Kitty" — surgically implanting listening devices into cats to spy on the Soviets.'),
  (944, 'Giraffes have the highest blood pressure of any mammal — needed to pump blood 2 meters up to their head.'),
  (945, 'A group of crows is called a murder; a group of crows and ravens together is sometimes called a conspiracy.'),
  (946, 'The heart of a shrimp is inside its head.'),
  (947, 'The first ice cream cone was invented at the 1904 World''s Fair in St. Louis when a waffle vendor started rolling waffles to hold ice cream for a neighboring vendor who had run out of dishes.'),
  (948, 'Humans are the only animals that keep other animals as pets.'),
  (949, 'Tardigrades (water bears) have survived five mass extinctions — they''ve been on Earth for over 500 million years.'),
  (950, 'Humpback whales teach each other songs — new calls spread from whale to whale across populations, like music trends.'),
  (951, 'The footprint of Neil Armstrong on the Moon''s surface will outlast the pyramids of Giza.'),
  (952, 'There are more stars in the observable universe than all the grains of sand on Earth''s beaches.'),
  (953, 'Hamsters can run up to 8 miles a night on their wheel.'),
  (954, 'Iceland generates 100% of its electricity from renewable sources — primarily geothermal and hydropower.'),
  (955, 'The word "salary" and the saying "worth his salt" come from the historical use of salt as currency by Roman soldiers.'),
  (956, 'Most of the caffeine in decaf coffee comes from residual traces, not none at all — a "decaf" can still contain 10–15 mg of caffeine.'),
  (957, 'The fingerprints of gorillas are individual and distinguishable, like human fingerprints.'),
  (958, 'The Great Barrier Reef spans more than 2,300 kilometers, making it visible from space.'),
  (959, 'Hummingbirds are the only birds that can fly backwards.'),
  (960, 'There are approximately 7.5 quintillion grains of sand on Earth''s beaches.'),
  (961, 'The process of continental drift has brought all the continents together and then separated them multiple times in Earth''s history.'),
  (962, 'A shrimp''s heart is located in its head.'),
  (963, 'The moon''s gravity slows Earth''s rotation — a day on Earth was once only about 6 hours long.'),
  (964, 'Most commercial honey bees in North America are transported cross-country by truck to pollinate crops.'),
  (965, 'Bowhead whales can live to over 200 years old, and many alive today may have been born before the US Civil War.'),
  (966, 'The male emperor penguin holds its egg on its feet under a flap of skin for 65 days during winter, never eating — losing almost half its body weight.'),
  (967, 'Albert Einstein''s autopsy report stated his brain was 15% smaller than average — yet with significantly denser neural connections in certain areas.'),
  (968, 'More data is created every two days today than was created in all of human history from the dawn of civilization up to 2003.'),
  (969, 'A grizzly bear can smell food from 18 miles away.'),
  (970, 'In Alaska, it is illegal to be drunk in a bar. The bartender is responsible for informing the police.'),
  (971, 'Peanut butter was used in NASA astronaut food rations for Apollo missions.'),
  (972, 'Japan has an estimated 3,000 Michelin-starred restaurants — more than any other country in the world.'),
  (973, 'The country of Nauru is the world''s smallest island country, at just 21 square kilometers.'),
  (974, 'You can identify the sex of most sea turtles by which way they circle in a tank — females circle counterclockwise, males clockwise.'),
  (975, 'The largest spider in the world, the Goliath birdeater, is so large it occasionally catches and eats small birds.'),
  (976, 'Identical twins have slightly different DNA — mutations accumulate throughout their lives, making them genetically distinct despite starting identical.'),
  (977, 'The word "utopia" means "no place" in Greek — Sir Thomas More coined it in 1516 to describe an ideal but imaginary society.'),
  (978, 'The odds that a coin will land heads or tails is not perfectly 50/50 — the weight distribution of a coin is slightly uneven.'),
  (979, 'Cats are responsible for the extinction of at least 33 bird species worldwide, primarily on islands.'),
  (980, 'Earth''s oxygen supply is renewed entirely by photosynthesis — all the oxygen we breathe was once carbon dioxide.'),
  (981, 'Lightning can heat the surrounding air to temperatures five times hotter than the surface of the Sun.'),
  (982, 'There is more empty space in the human body than solid matter — because atoms are mostly empty.'),
  (983, 'A hippo can open its mouth to a 150-degree angle — wide enough to fit a 4-foot child inside.'),
  (984, 'The world''s first photograph took 8 hours to expose, taken by Joseph Nicéphore Niépce in 1826.'),
  (985, 'Stars don''t actually twinkle — it''s atmospheric turbulence that makes their light appear to shimmer.'),
  (986, 'Polar bears aren''t actually white — they look white because their hollow fur scatters visible light.'),
  (987, 'The Amazon River discharges about 20% of all fresh water that flows into the world''s oceans.'),
  (988, 'There are 10^18 (a quintillion) insects alive on Earth at any given moment.'),
  (989, 'Cheetahs make chirping and purring sounds — not roars. They''re the only big cat that can''t roar.'),
  (990, 'In 1977, a signal of unknown origin was detected by a radio telescope — lasting 72 seconds. It was dubbed the "Wow! signal" and has never been explained or detected again.'),
  (991, 'Venus spins backwards compared to most planets — if you stood on Venus, the Sun would rise in the west.'),
  (992, 'Bowhead whales are the longest-lived mammals, with some estimated to have lived over 200 years.'),
  (993, 'The "plastic" in plastic surgery doesn''t refer to synthetic materials — it comes from the Greek word "plastikos," meaning to mold or shape.'),
  (994, 'The human immune system kills roughly one million cancer cells every day without us ever knowing.'),
  (995, 'Ants navigate partly by counting their own footsteps — desert ants can measure distances with remarkable accuracy.'),
  (996, 'There are more possible board positions in the game of Go than there are atoms in the observable universe.'),
  (997, 'Humans shed about 600,000 particles of skin every hour — over a lifetime, we shed enough to fill an entire house.'),
  (998, 'Scientists have discovered water ice at the poles of Mercury, despite its scorching surface temperatures.'),
  (999, 'Humpback whales have complex "songs" that can last up to 20 minutes and evolve across the population each year.'),
  (1000, 'Every hydrogen atom in your body is approximately 13.8 billion years old — as old as the universe itself.'),
  (1001, 'The shortest commercial flight in the world takes off, reaches cruising altitude, and lands — all in under 2 minutes.'),
  (1002, 'There are approximately 7 billion humans alive right now, and statistically, every single one of them believes they are slightly better than average at driving.')
